<!--
  Camcorder/encoder profiles for k50sv1_64_bsp. Written by the device tree, not
  extracted, because Stock's copy is wrong in two ways that both reach the user.

  1. It declared highspeedlow / highspeedhigh / highspeed480p as 640x480 @ 120 fps.
     Neither camera can do it: dumpsys media.camera reports, for BOTH devices,
     preview-fps-range-values (15000,15000),(20000,20000),(24000,24000),
     (5000,30000),(30000,30000) - a 30 fps ceiling - and there is no HFR or
     video-hsr parameter at all. CamcorderProfile.hasProfile(QUALITY_HIGH_SPEED_*)
     is answered purely from this file, so an app that offers slow motion on that
     basis fails at record time. The three profiles are removed.

  2. VideoEncoderCap for h264 carried maxFrameHeight="1088".
     StagefrightRecorder::checkVideoEncoderCapabilities() -> clipVideoFrameHeight()
     CLAMPS rather than fails, on every non-time-lapse session, so a MediaRecorder
     configured for this panel's 720x1560 was silently recorded at 720x1088 and
     scaled - vertically squashed video, on the standard MediaProjection screen
     recording path. Raised to 1920. The hardware really does 720x1560: see
     E-025, where screenrecord produced a valid 132-frame H.264 at that size.

  NOT touched, deliberately: media_codecs_mediatek_video.xml gives
  OMX.MTK.VIDEO.ENCODER.AVC a size Limit of max="1920x1080", which looks like it
  should reject 720x1560 and does not. The same block carries
  <Feature name="can-swap-width-height">, so MediaCodecInfo extends both ranges
  to 96..1920 and sets mSmallerDimensionUpperLimit = min(1920,1080) = 1080;
  supports(720,1560) then passes because 720 <= 1080. Raising or removing that
  max would change mSmallerDimensionUpperLimit and could break what works.

  cameraId 0's high/1080p/timelapse1080p at 1920x1080 are real - video-size-values
  for device 0 includes 1920x1080. cameraId 1's 720p ceiling is conservative but
  safe. Both left alone.
-->
